{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>Eat it today, etc.</b> One needs to know why it was necessary to command them about eating it. And perhaps it is because, since He told them nothing but 'leave it for safekeeping' and did not specify to them that they would be permitted to eat it — even though we do not find that He explicitly forbade eating the leftover — from this you learn that He had forbidden it to them. Or perhaps it is forbidden by the rule of 'you shall not eat any abominable thing,' and they said, of blessed memory (Ḥullin 114a): anything I have declared an abomination to you falls under 'you shall not eat it'; therefore He said to them, <b>eat it today.</b> And He was precise in saying <b>today</b>, to exclude another day — the prohibition of the leftover applies until morning. And in order that you not err and say 'today' specifically and not another day at all, even on Shabbat — therefore He concluded by saying <b>for it is Shabbat</b> etc. From this you have learned that the specification of 'today' comes not to restrict but to indicate that it is not Shabbat." "<b>Further,</b> He further intended by saying <b>for it is Shabbat</b> a reason for what follows after it in this manner — for the reason that on the day of Shabbat you will not find it in the field. He was precise in saying <b>in the field</b> to allude to the statement of Rabbi Shimon ben Yoḥai, who said in the Zohar (vol. II, 88a) that on the day of Shabbat the sustenance of all six days descends from above, and from that sustenance a daily portion comes down each day. And that is what He says — <b>you will not find it in the field</b> — but above, there it will be found; on the contrary, sustenance for all six days."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:28:53.563987901Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}